Muriel set out two cups.

The plum tree by the gate had flowered in the night. It was the wrong season for it. White blossom stood on every black twig, and behind it the lane was gray, and the barracks roofs were gray, and the sky. She looked at it through the window for some time with the kettle in her hand.

"Daft thing," she said.

She poured his first. He took it strong. She could not find his good cup, the blue one with the mend in it, and had not been able to find it for a long while now, so he would have to make do with the brown. She told the empty chair so. She told it the tea was on the table and would not keep. Then she sat down across from it and drank her own, and watched the steam go out of his.

It had been a strange morning. Someone had left a loaf on her step, still warm in its cloth. At the well, women she hardly knew had nodded to her with soft, careful faces, as though she were ill. And in the square there had been something missing. She had stood in the mud with her bucket trying to think what it was. The posts were there. The beam was there. The wind went through it all and nothing rattled. Young Matthis was not there either, who was always there.

She hoped he was not poorly. He never did come for his tea.

When his cup was cold she carried it out and emptied it on the roots of the plum, as she did every day, so as not to waste it. Petals came down on her wrists. On the gatepost stood a blue cup full of rainwater, green at the rim, with one white petal turning on the top of it. She went past it, and in, and shut the door.

--

Before that, there was the year.

She walked to the square each day when the noon bell rang. Most days she went for the air, and the square was only the square, with something built in the middle of it that her eye slid over, as it slid over the barracks and the coal carts and everything else that had not been there when she was a girl. On those days she stopped to talk with young Matthis. He looked so tired. She told him to come by for a cuppa, and he said yes, Aunty Muriel, and she went home pleased.

There were other days, when she remembered. Those were the bad days.

On those days she saw what hung there. Fourth cage from the left. She would have known the set of those shoulders in the dark. She beat on the Bone Man's chest with both her fists and called him every name she had, and he stood and took it. He never once put up his hands. When her arms gave out he held her by the elbows until the remembering passed, and then he said hello, Goody Muriel, as if she had only that moment come.

She was always a little short of breath, walking home. She could not think why.

The cup stood on the gatepost. Snow filled it, then rain.

--

Before that, there was the night the dead came down the hill.

She was awake. She had not slept that week. He was seven days in the ground and she had counted each of them. So when boots came down the lane she knew them. He had always landed hard on his left heel. She heard the gate. She heard the latch lift twice, the way he lifted it when he was late home and she had barred the door, and she was out of bed with her shawl half on before she remembered where he was.

She stood on the cold flags in the dark. The latch lifted again.

She put the kettle on.

Then there were torches in the lane, and men shouting, and something heavy went away from the door very fast. She sat by the hearth until the kettle boiled dry. In the morning there were four long furrows in the oak, at the height of a man's hand. She sanded them out. It took until dusk. She oiled the wood afterward so the pale place would not show, and she did not bar the door that night, or any night after.

The cup stood on the gatepost with ten days of dust in it.

--

Before that, there was the hill.

Twenty-two graves in three rows, cut so fresh the dust had not settled. The Vicar read the names. He read them slowly, and after each one somebody in the crowd made a sound. The sun was out. She remembered thinking it had no business to be. It lay bright on Osric's marker, which she had rubbed with beeswax the night before until she could see the candle in it.

Young Matthis stood off by the wall with the dust still gray in his hair. He stood as though he were ashamed to be standing. She went and took his arm. One of them needed holding up, and she was not certain which.

"You'll call me Aunty," she told him. "I've asked you often enough."

On the way home she came to the gatepost. The cup was where he had left it. She stopped, and put out her hand.

Then she took her hand back and went in.

--

Before that, there was a morning.

There was nothing in it. That was what she could not forgive. Porridge. A sock that wanted darning. He ate standing, and she told him to sit, and he bent and kissed the top of her head on his way past. He had been able to do that since he was fourteen and had never got tired of it.

He took his tea out with him. He drank it at the gate with his face turned up to the weather, and set the cup on the post, and went up the lane with the others toward the Pit.

Twenty years she had been fetching that cup in. That morning she stood in the doorway with her arms folded and thought, let him fetch it himself for once.

The washing was half on the line when the bell began.

--

Before that, there were two years of money.

The men came over the pass from Moonfall Hollow with picks on their shoulders, more men than she had seen in her life, and inside a month there were ten of them for every face she knew. They cut the linden in the square because the carts could not turn around it. They cut the orchard on the south hill for pit props. The brook where she had rinsed his first shirts ran black by midsummer and did not run clear again.

The Guild paid a wizard three times what a village could. He came home with coal in the creases of his hands and laid the coins on the table one at a time.

"It's good money, Mam."

She said nothing. He bought her a window of real glass with it. She washed that window every morning, and by evening she could have written her name on it.

The blue cup hung on its hook by the hearth, and came down every dawn.

--

Before that, there was the valley.

A hundred and fifty souls, and a church with one bell. From her gate she could put a name to every chimney. Osric did their mending. Plowshares, kettles, the mill wheel twice. His mending always left a little something over, as mending does, and the valley kept what was left, the same as it kept its three-legged dogs. The smith's fence post hummed in a north wind. The clover behind the church came up blue. The millstone stayed warm all winter, and the miller's wife set her dough to rise on it, and the children sat with their backs against it in the snow.

Nobody called them effects then. They called them Osric's leavings, and showed them to visitors.

Young Matthis came on Sundays and ate plum jam off the spoon when he thought she was not looking. Osric drank from the blue cup with the mend in it. He would not use another.

--

Before that, there was a boy of seven, standing very still in the kitchen with half a blue cup in each hand.

It had been her mother's. He knew that. His chin was going. She had her mouth open to tell him it was only a cup when he shut his eyes and pushed the halves together, hard, the way a child pushes who has never wanted anything so much.

When he opened his hands it was whole. A line ran down it as fine as a hair.

Neither of them spoke. Outside, in October, the plum tree by the gate came into flower all at once, every twig of it, and the bees came out of the church wall to see.

"Well," she said, when she could. "That one's yours, then."

--

Before that, there was a spring morning with the door standing open.

The orchard on the south hill was white from top to bottom. The brook was loud with snowmelt, and so clear she could have counted the stones in it from the step. By the gate a plum whip no taller than the post stood tied to its stake. There was not a black thing in the whole valley but the rooks.

She was three days out of childbed and had been told to stay in it. She was at the hearth regardless, with the kettle on and her son in the crook of her arm. He was awake. He was looking up at her with great seriousness. His eyes were going to be brown. She could tell already.

She took down her cup with her free hand. Then she looked at him, and laughed at herself, and took down another.

"That's for when you've teeth," she told him.

Muriel set out two cups.
